  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Colour Blocks",
    "overview": "The answer arrives as three large flat blocks that can be read in seconds: before, after, and the change. The palette is a warm sunset on a white ground with plum as the ink. Corners are large, controls are pill-shaped, and one typeface carries everything that isn't SQL. Nothing decorative stands between a developer and the numbers.\n\nIt is fun through colour and scale, and calm through restraint: one theme (light) on every screen, no gradients, no charts, flat fills. Colour has jobs. It says which side of the experiment a number belongs to, and which verdict the result is allowed to claim. A word always says the same thing.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Big tabular numbers in flat colour blocks; no charts or diagrams on results.",
      "Peach is before, amber is after, coral is the primary action and a faster verdict, plum fill is slower.",
      "Colour follows the verdict, and every verdict is also written out.",
      "Large radii and pill controls; depth only on things that float.",
      "Hanken Grotesk throughout; monospace only for SQL."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Before And After Rule",
        "body": "Peach means before and amber means after. No other colour takes either role, and neither one fills a data block that isn't that side. Counts across a workload are neither, so their blocks are blush.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Verdict Colour Rule",
        "body": "Colour follows the verdict, never the sign of a number. Faster is coral (in tables, plum figures beside a coral badge). Slower is a plum fill with white text; rust text is only for results that count as slower, and for errors. A change that doesn't count is soft plum in result and candidate tables, whichever way its arrow points.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Say It Too Rule",
        "body": "Every verdict is also a word: \"Faster\", \"Slower\", \"No real change\" or \"No real difference\", \"Too few runs to tell\". Change figures carry an arrow.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No Claim Rule",
        "body": "With too few runs to check against variation, no direction is claimed, even if the server marked one. The result reads \"Too few runs to tell\", takes the neutral treatment, and stays out of regression lists and counts.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Median Takes No Side Rule",
        "body": "A median across several queries is never timing-checked, so it never takes a verdict colour. Its block is blush with a plum ring, and median figures in lists and comparisons stay plum.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Typeface Rule",
        "body": "Identifiers, table and column names, timings and plan node names stay in Hanken Grotesk, set apart by weight 600. Monospace means SQL and nothing else.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Fill Rule",
        "body": "Blocks, cards, panels and buttons cast no shadow and take no gradient, at rest or on hover.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do keep peach for before and amber for after wherever the two sides of a result are shown as colour fills.",
      "Do derive colour from the verdict and print the verdict as a word beside it.",
      "Do give \"no real difference\", too few runs, A/A checks and workload medians the neutral treatment.",
      "Do show how far to trust a result next to the result.",
      "Do use plum text on coral, amber and peach, and white text on plum.",
      "Do stack tables on phones with each value labelled, so no verdict is off screen.",
      "Do meet WCAG 2.1 AA, with a visible plum focus outline."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't put charts, sparklines or timing diagrams on results; show the numbers.",
      "Don't colour a figure because it went up or down.",
      "Don't claim a direction from too few runs.",
      "Don't use rust text for anything but results that count as slower, and errors.",
      "Don't set anything but SQL in monospace.",
      "Don't put soft plum text on coral or amber; it falls below 4.5:1.",
      "Don't add gradients, or shadows to surfaces that don't float.",
      "Don't break the answer into a dashboard of small stat cards."
    ]
  }
}